9. My recommended priorities for you

Considering the casino audit and the associated delivery responsibilities, I would organize your immediate efforts into three priorities.

01

Establish the report’s defensible baseline

Review the AML program, historical procedures, risk assessment, monitoring methods, and earlier recommendations. Separate control design from implementation and operating effectiveness.

02

Develop the OFAC incident assessment

Construct an exact chronology, determine root causes, examine the disclosure and regulator correspondence, and test whether remediation addresses the underlying control failure.

03

Evaluate whether the compliance program can support expansion

Assess staffing, transactional volume, surveillance cooperation, delayed procurement, unresolved findings and escalation channels as a connected operating risk.

Additional points I would not overlook

There are a few issues that received relatively little attention during the call but deserve more scrutiny.

First, determine whether the OFAC incident requires more than a procedural finding. It may reveal shortcomings across the three lines of defense: frontline handling, supervisory oversight, and independent monitoring.

Second, look for recurring themes across previous audits. The client referenced prior findings and continued delays with systems and surveillance. A recommendation repeated over several years without effective closure can be more significant than a newly identified weakness.

Third, distinguish regulatory delays from management accountability. Even if approvals and procurement are delayed, the audit should establish what interim controls management implemented.

Fourth, consider a targeted expansion-readiness assessment. The new gaming floor, additional machines, and growing transaction activity create a natural reason to review whether the existing AML program remains appropriately designed and adequately resourced.

Fifth, preserve a consistent audit cutoff date. Historical 2025 evidence, the September 2026 OFAC incident, newly implemented controls, and December 2026 planned improvements should be clearly distinguished. Otherwise, the report may inadvertently treat future remediation commitments as completed controls.

